WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.700
Humans might have built civilizations,

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.440
but insects own the world.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.390
After all, over half of all
known species are insects.

00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:14.470
So if they all suddenly
vanished, you'd notice.

00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:18.780
No more summers of singing
cicadas and flickering fireflies.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:23.150
No bees to pollinate apple,
cherry, peach, or almond trees.

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:25.100
No one to make honey.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:27.500
A world without insects means a world

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:30.170
with empty grocery-store shelves.

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:33.510
But that would be just the
beginning of our problems.

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:37.660
Now, it's impossible to say
exactly what would go down,

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:41.660
but here's a worst-case
scenario of what could happen

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:43.873
if all the insects disappeared.

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:48.340
There are a few insects most people

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:50.330
would be happy to see vanish.

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:51.710
Like mosquitoes.

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:55.290
They kill hundreds of
thousands of people every year

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:58.160
by transmitting malaria, West Nile virus,

00:00:58.160 --> 00:00:59.810
and other diseases.

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:01.700
But if they disappeared tomorrow,

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:04.000
we might actually miss them.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.940
There are over 3,000 species
of mosquitoes on Earth,

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:09.780
all of which are food to birds,

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:12.150
bats, frogs, and other animals.

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.870
No more mosquitoes means these creatures

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:18.430
and the animals that eat
them could go hungry.

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:21.050
The same goes for the dreaded cockroach,

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:23.560
a protein-packed meal for birds, rodents,

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:26.560
and even humans in some
parts of the world.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:30.940
If we lost all 4,400 species of roach,

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:34.540
entire ecosystems would
struggle to survive.

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:38.180
Believe it or not, we'd have
even worse troubles ahead

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:41.270
since we'd face a serious poop problem

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:44.490
without one of the world's
greatest recyclers,

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:46.010
the dung beetle.

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:49.477
You see, history has taught
us exactly what happens

00:01:49.477 --> 00:01:52.090
when these critters can't do their job.

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:57.090
Back in 1788, the British
introduced cattle to Australia,

00:01:57.610 --> 00:02:00.200
and these cows pooped a lot.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:04.610
Each one poops enough to fill
five tennis courts every year.

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:06.840
But while the dung beetles back in Britain

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:09.150
would eat and break down cow poo,

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:12.750
the native Australian beetles
wouldn't touch the stuff

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:14.275
because they evolved to munch

00:02:14.275 --> 00:02:17.740
only on dry, fibrous marsupial dung.

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:20.400
So the cow poop piled up.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:25.390
By 1960, the cattle had
carpeted 500,000 acres

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:27.620
of pasture in dung.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:30.890
That's enough to cover
over half of Rhode Island,

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:34.140
and while a little bit of
poop is great for fertilizer,

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:38.320
this ocean of dung would
flood plants with nitrogen,

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.780
making it impossible for anything to grow.

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:45.600
So, imagine if all 8,000
species of dung beetle,

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.460
plus other doo-dining insects,

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:50.500
like flies, vanished worldwide.

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:53.680
The land would be knee-deep in...you know.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:58.010
Farmland, forest, and
desert would all collapse,

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:01.793
and floating throughout
would be loads of corpses.

00:03:01.793 --> 00:03:05.900
You see, most animals
won't eat dead bodies.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:09.410
That's where flesh-eating
beetles, aka dermestids,

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:12.390
and other corpse-munching insects come in.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:17.390
Over 500 species of these grisly
undertakers live worldwide,

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:21.770
devouring dead flesh until
nothing but bone remains.

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:24.912
Without them, there would
be fewer custodians around

00:03:24.912 --> 00:03:26.310
to clean up the mess.

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:28.940
Sure, there would still be hungry vultures

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:31.000
and bacteria around to help,

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:34.030
but it wouldn't be enough.

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:38.050
So, that's where we could end
up in an insect-less world.

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:40.210
Starving to death while drowning

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:42.673
in a sea of poop and corpses.

